== Mathematical Origin ==

=== Coupled Mode Theory ===

Let <math>a_1(z)</math> and <math>a_2(z)</math> be the complex amplitudes of the guided ψ modes in channels 1 and 2. When the channels are brought close together, the evanescent tail of each mode overlaps with the other, inducing a coupling. The coupled mode equations:

:<math>\frac{da_1}{dz} = -i\kappa\,a_2 e^{i\Delta\beta z}</math>
:<math>\frac{da_2}{dz} = -i\kappa\,a_1 e^{-i\Delta\beta z}</math>

where <math>\kappa</math> is the coupling coefficient and <math>\Delta\beta = k_{z1} - k_{z2}</math> is the phase mismatch. For identical channels (<math>\Delta\beta = 0</math>), the solution with input <math>a_1(0) = 1</math>, <math>a_2(0) = 0</math>:

:<math>a_1(z) = \cos(\kappa z)</math>
:<math>a_2(z) = -i\sin(\kappa z)</math>

Power in each channel:

:<math>P_1(z) = \cos^2(\kappa z), \qquad P_2(z) = \sin^2(\kappa z)</math>

Complete energy transfer at <math>z = \pi/(2\kappa)</math>. The 3 dB (equal split) length:

:<math>\boxed{L_{3\rm dB} = \frac{\pi}{4\kappa}}</math>

=== Coupling Coefficient ===

The coupling coefficient is the overlap integral between the evanescent tail of mode 1 and the core of waveguide 2:

:<math>\kappa = \frac{\omega}{4P}\int_{\rm channel\,2} \Delta\varepsilon_\psi(\mathbf{x})\,\psi_1^*(\mathbf{x})\,\psi_2(\mathbf{x})\,d^2x</math>

where <math>\Delta\varepsilon_\psi</math> is the permittivity contrast between the channel core and the gap material. For practical TPMS waveguides separated by gap <math>d</math>, the coupling coefficient scales as:

:<math>\kappa \propto e^{-\kappa_{\rm evan}\,d}, \qquad \kappa_{\rm evan} = \sqrt{\omega_c^2 - \omega^2}</math>

At the [[Psi Stabilizer]] operating frequency (40 kHz), well above waveguide cutoff, <math>\kappa_{\rm evan}</math> is small — the evanescent tail decays slowly. This means coupling occurs over large gaps, but it also means κ is sensitive to large gap variations. At 40 kHz with 2 cm channel: <math>\kappa_{\rm evan} \approx \sqrt{\omega_c^2 - \omega^2} \approx \sqrt{(16\,\text{kHz})^2 - (40\,\text{kHz})^2} \cdot (2\pi/c)</math> — imaginary (because 40 kHz > 16 kHz cutoff), confirming propagating mode. The evanescent coupling gap must be below cutoff — requires a gap narrower than the channel.

== Implementation ==

In practice, ψ couplers for PsiNet are implemented as two parallel TPMS channels separated by a gap of 1–5 mm, with coupling length set to achieve the desired power split ratio. The gap region contains a higher-m_eff material (higher ferrofluid H-field, from [[ψ Permeability]] B3) to ensure the gap is in the evanescent regime relative to the guided mode.

'''Gap fabrication tolerance:''' A ±10 μm variation in gap d produces approximately ±5% variation in κ (from the exponential sensitivity), giving ±5% variation in power split at the 3 dB point — acceptable for most PsiNet applications.

'''Directional coupler (4-port):''' Two parallel TPMS channels of length <math>L_{3\rm dB}</math> in close proximity form a 4-port directional coupler. Signal entering port 1 splits equally between port 2 (through) and port 3 (coupled), with zero output at port 4 (isolated) — the standard directional coupler property that enables PsiNet signal routing.

== ψ-Dependent Extensions ==

The operator-to-operator psionic coupling in a [[PsiNet]] group session is modelled as a biological implementation of evanescent coupling. Each operator's biological ψ field (from [[ψ Source]] C11) has a spatial extent set by <math>L_\psi^{\rm (bio)}</math>. When two operators are within distance <math>2L_\psi^{\rm (bio)}</math> of each other, their biological ψ fields overlap in the evanescent region — transferring ψ energy between them exactly as the device coupler does between channels. The effective biological coupling coefficient:

:<math>\kappa_{\rm bio} \propto e^{-d_{\rm operators}/L_\psi^{\rm (bio)}}</math>

At typical social distance (1–2 m) and <math>L_\psi^{\rm (bio)} = 20</math> cm: <math>\kappa_{\rm bio} \propto e^{-5}</math> to <math>e^{-10}</math> — very weak. This predicts that direct biological ψ coupling requires close physical proximity, consistent with traditional accounts of group psionic practice.

